WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.960
You may have heard the phrase "The Unreasonable&nbsp;
Effectiveness of Mathematics in the Natural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:08.640
Sciences." This was the title of a paper&nbsp;
by the physicist Eugene Wigner but even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:13.140
more fun than the title is the way that he&nbsp;
chooses to open it. The paper begins quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:17.820
"There is a story about two friends who were&nbsp;
classmates in high school..." talking about their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:21.840
jobs. One of them became a statistician&nbsp;
and was working on population trends.

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:26.520
They showed a reprint to their former classmates&nbsp;
and the reprint started as usual with the Gausian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:30.840
distribution. The statistician explained&nbsp;
to the former classmate the meaning of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:35.880
the symbols for the actual population, the&nbsp;
average population and so on. The classmate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:40.740
was a incredulous and was not quite sure whether&nbsp;
the statistician was pulling their leg. "How can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:45.780
you know that?" was the query and "What is this&nbsp;
symbol over here?" "Oh", said the statistician.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:46.980
"This is pi."

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:47.940
"What is that?"

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:51.240
"The ratio of the circumference&nbsp;
of a circle to its diameter."

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:54.540
"Well, now you're pushing the joke&nbsp;
too far.", said the classmate.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:57.900
"Surely, the population has nothing to&nbsp;
do with the circumference of a circle."

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:02.640
In the paper, Wigner then goes on to&nbsp;
talk about the more general phenomenon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:06.960
of concepts in pure math seeming to find&nbsp;
applications that extend strangely beyond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:10.920
what their definitions would suggest.&nbsp;
But I would like to stay focused on this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:14.580
particular anecdote and the question that&nbsp;
the statistician's friend is getting at.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:18.600
You see, there is a very beautiful&nbsp;
and classic proof that explains the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:22.320
pi inside the formula for a normal&nbsp;
distribution and despite there being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:26.100
a number of other really great explanations&nbsp;
online, see some links in the description,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:29.760
I cannot help but indulge in the&nbsp;
pleasure of reanimating it here.

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:33.720
For one thing, there is a fun side note&nbsp;
that I didn't learn until recently about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:37.500
how you can use this proof to derive the&nbsp;
volumes of higher dimensional spheres.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:43.140
But much more importantly than that, what I really&nbsp;
want to do is try to go beyond the classic proof.

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:47.460
Consider this hypothetical statistician's&nbsp;
friend. What I want to ask is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:51.540
can we find an explanation that would&nbsp;
satisfy their disbelief? You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:55.200
they're not just asking for some pure math&nbsp;
proof about a function that was handed down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.200 --> 00:02:00.840
to them on high. The friend's incredulity was that&nbsp;
circles should have anything to do with population&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:06.480
statistics. Until we fully draw that connecting&nbsp;
line, we should consider the task incomplete.

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:10.320
Those of you who watched the last video on&nbsp;
the Central Limit Theorem will have some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:14.340
of the backdrop here because there we broke&nbsp;
down the formula for a normal distribution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:19.080
which is also called a Gaussian Distribution&nbsp;
and when you strip away all of the different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:23.040
parameters and the constants, the basic&nbsp;
function that describes the bell curve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:30.600
shape is e^-x^2. And the reason that pi showed up&nbsp;
in the final formula was that the area underneath&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:35.520
this curve works out, as you will see in a&nbsp;
couple minutes, to be the square root of pi.

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:39.660
So, what that meant for us was that at&nbsp;
some point we needed to divide out by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:42.720
that square root of pi to make sure&nbsp;
that the area under the curve is 1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:46.680
which is a requirement before you can&nbsp;
interpret it as a probability distribution.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:52.500
In the full formula that you would see, say in a&nbsp;
stats book, this gets mixed together with some of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:58.200
the other constants. But in its purest form, that&nbsp;
pi originates from the area underneath this curve.

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:04.620
So, step number one for you and me is to explain&nbsp;
that area. But I want to emphasize it's not the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:09.120
last step. To satisfy the question raised&nbsp;
by that hypothetical statistician's friend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:12.540
we need to go further. We&nbsp;
need to also answer why is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:15.900
it that this function e^-x^2 from&nbsp;
is so special in the first place.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:20.340
I mean, there are lots of different formulas&nbsp;
you could write down that would give a shape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:24.060
that you know, vaguely bulges in the&nbsp;
middle and tapers out on either side.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:29.580
So why is it that this specific function&nbsp;
holds such a special place in statistics?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:34.620
To phrase our goal another way - can we find&nbsp;
a connection between the proof that shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:39.360
why pi shows up and the Central Limit Theorem&nbsp;
which, as we talked about in the last video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:44.160
is the thing that explains when you can expect&nbsp;
a normal distribution to arise in nature.

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:48.060
So, with all of that is the goal, first&nbsp;
things first, let's dig into the classic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:54.840
and very beautiful proof. When you want&nbsp;
to find the area underneath the curve,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:59.520
the tool for doing that is an integral. As a quick&nbsp;
reminder for how you might read this notation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:03.360
you might imagine approximating that area&nbsp;
with many different rectangles under the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:07.860
curve where the height of each such rectangle&nbsp;
is the value of the function above that point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:13.920
in this case e^-x^2 for a certain input x and the&nbsp;
width is some little number that we're calling dx.

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:18.720
We need to add up the areas of all these&nbsp;
rectangles for values of x ranging from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:23.580
negative infinity up to infinity and the use&nbsp;
of that notation dx is kind of meant to imply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:27.720
you shouldn't think of any specific width but&nbsp;
instead you ask - as the chosen width for your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:32.040
rectangle gets thinner and thinner, what&nbsp;
does this sum of all those areas approach?

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:36.000
Of course, all of that is just notation&nbsp;
unless you provide a way to answer that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:41.280
question and the magic of calculus is that it&nbsp;
provides just that. At least usually. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:46.140
usually the procedure here would be to find some&nbsp;
function whose derivative is equal to the stuff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:51.900
we have on the inside, e^-x^2. In other words, we&nbsp;
want to find an antiderivative of that function.

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:57.480
The problem is, for this particular function,&nbsp;
it is provably not possible to find such an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:00.960
antiderivative. It's a little weird&nbsp;
and beyond the scope of what I want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:04.620
to talk about here. But basically, even&nbsp;
though there exists an antiderivative,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:10.020
it is a well-defined function. You cannot express&nbsp;
what that antiderivative is using all our usual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:14.580
tools like polynomial expressions, trig functions,&nbsp;
exponentials or any way to mix them together.

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:18.720
So, finding this area requires a bit of&nbsp;
cleverness. There needs to be a new trick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:22.080
that we bring to bear. And the first&nbsp;
step to this trick is easily the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:26.220
absurd. We start by bumping things up one&nbsp;
dimension, so that instead of asking for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:31.500
the area under a bell curve, we ask for the&nbsp;
volume underneath this kind of bell surface.

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:36.300
You could rightly ask, "Why would you&nbsp;
do that? Who ordered another dimension?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:40.560
And I'll admit, it's not terribly motivated&nbsp;
right now other than to say watch what happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:45.180
when we just try it. And in general with hard&nbsp;
problems, it's never a bad idea to try solving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:48.780
"cousins" of the problem since that can help&nbsp;
you get a little bit of momentum and insight.

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:53.580
To be clear on how this higher dimensional&nbsp;
function is defined - it takes in two different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:58.140
inputs, X and Y, which we might think of as&nbsp;
a point on the XY plane and the way to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:03.000
about it is to consider the distance from that&nbsp;
point to the origin, which I'll label as "r" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:07.440
then to plug in that distance to our original&nbsp;
bell curve function. That is, we take e^-r^2.

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:12.360
You might notice the lines I've drawn&nbsp;
on this diagram make a right triangle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:17.040
so by the Pythagorean theorem, x^2+y^2= r^2. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:20.940
in the function I have written where you&nbsp;
see x^2+y^2, you can think in the back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:24.660
of your mind that's really the square of&nbsp;
the distance from the point to the origin.

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:29.640
The main thing to notice here is how this gives&nbsp;
our function a kind of circular symmetry in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:34.740
sense that all of the inputs that sit on a given&nbsp;
circle have the same output. And so, when we graph&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:39.480
this function in three dimensions, it means&nbsp;
it has a rotational symmetry about the Z axis.

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:44.760
Math tends to reward you when you respect its&nbsp;
symmetries. So for a question of computing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:48.900
volume underneath the surface, what we're&nbsp;
going to do is respect that symmetry and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:53.520
imagine integrating together a bunch of thin&nbsp;
little cylinders underneath that surface.

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:58.740
Here, making this a little more quantitative,&nbsp;
let's focus on just one of those cylindrical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:03.120
shells, where its area is going to be the&nbsp;
circumference of that shell times the height.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:08.340
You might imagine it as something like the label&nbsp;
on a soup can that we can unwrap into a rectangle.

00:07:08.340 --> 00:07:12.840
The circumference of the cylinder, which is&nbsp;
the top side of that rectangle is going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:19.260
2 pi times the radius. And then the height of our&nbsp;
cylinder, the other side of our rectangle is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:23.040
height of the surface at this point, which&nbsp;
by definition is the value of our function&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:27.480
associated with that radius, which like I&nbsp;
said earlier, you can think of as e^-r^2.

00:07:29.220 --> 00:07:33.540
Now the real way you want to think about this is&nbsp;
to give that a little bit of thickness which will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:39.060
call "dr" so that the volume that it represents&nbsp;
is approximately that area we just looked at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:45.300
multiplied by this thickness, dr. Our task now&nbsp;
is to integrate together or add together all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:50.760
these different cylinders as our ranges between&nbsp;
zero and infinity. Or more precisely, we consider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:55.320
what happens as that thickness gets thinner and&nbsp;
thinner approaching zero and we add together the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:59.220
volumes of the many many many different thin&nbsp;
cylinders that sit underneath that curve.

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:04.860
You might think "This is just a&nbsp;
harder version of what we were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:07.920
looking at earlier. Three dimensions&nbsp;
should be more complicated than two."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:12.240
But actually, something very helpful has&nbsp;
happened. First let me clean up a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:17.340
by factoring the pi outside that integral. Now&nbsp;
the stuff inside that integral, having picked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:23.100
up this term "2r" does have an antiderivative.&nbsp;
We can now apply the usual tactics of calculus.

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:30.660
Specifically, that whole inside expression&nbsp;
is the derivative of -e^-r^2. And so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:35.220
those of comfortable with calculus know what to&nbsp;
do from here. We take that antiderivative and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:40.200
plug in the upper bound, which is negative&nbsp;
infinity squared and that gives us zero or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:43.500
speaking a little bit more precisely if&nbsp;
you consider the limit of this expression&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.500 --> 00:08:47.700
as the input approaches infinity, the&nbsp;
limiting value is zero and we subtract&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:52.560
off the value of that antiderivative at the&nbsp;
lower bound, zero, which in this case is -1.

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:57.480
So, all in all, the whole integral just works&nbsp;
out to be 1, which means all we're left with is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:03.060
that factor out in front, pi. Evidently, the&nbsp;
volume underneath this bell surface is pi.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:06.780
And I'll point out in this case it's not wild that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.780 --> 00:09:10.200
pi shows up because the surface has&nbsp;
this intrinsic circular symmetry.

00:09:10.860 --> 00:09:15.480
Still, you might wonder "How does that help&nbsp;
us?" As I said, throughout math, if you face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:20.640
a hard problem, solving an adjacent problem can&nbsp;
be unexpectedly helpful as a next step. And in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:24.866
this case it's helpful not just for building&nbsp;
intuition but we can directly relate the three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.866 --> 00:09:29.880
dimensional graph to our two dimensional graph by&nbsp;
analyzing the volume in a second different way.

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:35.460
You see, the more general way to approach&nbsp;
volumes underneath surfaces is to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.460 --> 00:09:39.840
of chopping it up into slices that are all&nbsp;
parallel to one of the axis. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:45.720
all these slices that are parallel to the&nbsp;
X axis. For example, this right here is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:50.460
a slice that corresponds to the plane y=0.&nbsp;
You might notice it looks just like a bell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:53.760
curve and if we write out the function,&nbsp;
this should actually make a lot of sense.

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:57.900
You could just plug in y=0, but to help&nbsp;
see what happens with other slices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:04.260
notice how thanks to the rules of exponentiation,&nbsp;
we could also write function as e^-x^2 * e^-y^2.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:12.240
It factors out nicely. On this slice, that e^-y^2,&nbsp;
is just a number - specifically the number 1.

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:18.120
So this is the same graph we've seen before,&nbsp;
e^-x^2, meaning that the area of this slice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.120 --> 00:10:22.740
is exactly the thing that we're looking for. It's&nbsp;
the mystery constant which I'm going to give the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:28.260
name "c". What's nice is there is nothing really&nbsp;
special about this particular slice. If we chose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:33.060
a different slice corresponding to a different Y&nbsp;
value, it corresponds to multiplying this curve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.060 --> 00:10:38.580
by a different number. So, it's the same basic&nbsp;
shape just scaled down by that number. Meaning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.580 --> 00:10:43.800
its area is the same as our mystery constant just&nbsp;
scaled down by some number. That's pretty cool.

00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:48.180
Each one of these slices has the same basic&nbsp;
shape just rescaled in the vertical direction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:53.040
Which by the way is not at all true&nbsp;
for most two variable functions. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:57.120
is very much dependent on the fact that&nbsp;
we were able to factor our function into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.120 --> 00:11:01.140
one part that's just dependent on the Y and&nbsp;
another part that's just dependent on the X.

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:05.160
Now, to think about the volume&nbsp;
underneath this whole surface,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:09.480
here's another way we could phrase it. We're&nbsp;
going to compute another integral that ranges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.480 --> 00:11:14.640
from Y equals negative infinity up to infinity&nbsp;
where the term inside that integral tells us the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:19.380
area of each one of those slices. And when&nbsp;
we multiply it by a little thickness, dy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.380 --> 00:11:22.740
you might think of it as giving each one&nbsp;
of those slices a little bit of volume.

00:11:22.740 --> 00:11:26.940
And remember, that term C sitting in&nbsp;
front represents the thing we want&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.940 --> 00:11:31.980
to know. Which itself is an integral. A&nbsp;
suspiciously similar looking integral.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.520 --> 00:11:36.360
See, if we take the expression on the&nbsp;
top and we factor out that constant C&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:40.500
because it's just a number, it doesn't&nbsp;
depend on Y, the thing we're left with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:44.820
the integral we need to compute is exactly&nbsp;
the mystery constant, the thing we don't know.

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:49.560
So, overall, the volume underneath&nbsp;
this bell surface works out to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.560 --> 00:11:54.660
this mystery constant squared. Out of&nbsp;
context, this might seem very unhelpful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.660 --> 00:11:59.460
it's just relating one thing we don't know to&nbsp;
another thing we don't know except we've already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.460 --> 00:12:04.140
computed the volume under this surface. We know&nbsp;
that it's equal to pi. Therefore, the mystery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.140 --> 00:12:09.000
constant we want to know, the area underneath&nbsp;
this bell curve, must be the square root of pi.

00:12:10.260 --> 00:12:15.480
It's a very pretty argument but a few things&nbsp;
are not entirely satisfying. For one thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:19.260
it feels a little bit like a trick,&nbsp;
something that just happened to work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.260 --> 00:12:23.760
without offering much of a sense for how you&nbsp;
could have rediscovered it yourself. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.760 --> 00:12:28.380
if we think back to our imagined statistician's&nbsp;
friend, it doesn't really answer their question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.380 --> 00:12:31.980
which was what do circles have&nbsp;
to do with population statistics.

00:12:32.520 --> 00:12:37.500
Like I said, it's the step, not the last and as&nbsp;
our next step, let's see if we can unpack why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:41.820
this proof is not quite as wild and arbitrary&nbsp;
as you might first think and how it relates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.820 --> 00:12:46.500
to an explanation for where this function&nbsp;
e^-x^2 is coming from in the first place.

00:12:49.320 --> 00:12:54.120
John Herschel was this&nbsp;
mathematician/scientist/inventor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.100 --> 00:13:01.140
who really did all sorts of things throughout the&nbsp;
19th century. He made contributions in chemistry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.140 --> 00:13:05.700
astronomy, photography, botany, he&nbsp;
invented the blueprint and named many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.700 --> 00:13:09.420
of the moons in our solar system and&nbsp;
in the midst of all of this, he also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.420 --> 00:13:13.980
offered a very elegant little derivation&nbsp;
for the Gaussian distribution in 1850.

00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:18.120
The setup is to imagine that you want&nbsp;
to describe some kind of probability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.120 --> 00:13:21.780
distribution in two dimensional space.&nbsp;
For instance maybe you want to model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.780 --> 00:13:26.100
the probability density for hits on&nbsp;
a dartboard. What Herschel showed is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.100 --> 00:13:30.120
that you want this distribution to satisfy&nbsp;
two pretty reasonable seaming properties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.120 --> 00:13:35.160
your hand is unexpectedly forced and even if&nbsp;
you had never heard of a Gaussian in your life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:40.680
you would be inexerably drawn to use a&nbsp;
function with the shape e^-x^2 + y^2.

00:13:40.680 --> 00:13:45.300
You do have 1° of freedom to control the&nbsp;
spread of that distribution and of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.300 --> 00:13:49.080
there's going to be some constant sitting&nbsp;
in front to make sure it's normalized but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.080 --> 00:13:52.740
the point is that we're forced into this&nbsp;
very specific kind of bell curve shape.

00:13:53.580 --> 00:13:57.360
The first of these two properties&nbsp;
is that probability density around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.360 --> 00:14:03.000
each point depends only on its distance&nbsp;
from the origin not on its direction. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.000 --> 00:14:06.420
on a dartboard with everybody aiming&nbsp;
for the bull's eye, this would mean that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.420 --> 00:14:09.420
you could rotate the board and it would&nbsp;
make no difference for the distribution.

00:14:10.440 --> 00:14:16.380
Mathematically, this means that the function&nbsp;
describing your probability distribution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.380 --> 00:14:21.480
which I'll call "f2" since it takes in two&nbsp;
inputs, X and Y. Well, it can be expressed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:27.720
as some single variable function of the radius,&nbsp;
r. And just to spell it out, r is the distance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.720 --> 00:14:31.920
between the point xy and the origin, the&nbsp;
square root of X squared plus Y squared.

00:14:32.940 --> 00:14:37.860
Property number 2 is that the X and Y coordinates&nbsp;
of each point are independent from each other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.860 --> 00:14:42.300
Which is to say, if you learn the X coordinate&nbsp;
of a point, it would give you no information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.300 --> 00:14:48.720
about the Y coordinate. The way this looks as an&nbsp;
equation is that our function which describes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:53.760
probability density around each point on the XY&nbsp;
plane can be factored into two different parts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.760 --> 00:14:58.200
One of which can be purely written in terms of&nbsp;
X. This is the distribution of the X coordinate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.200 --> 00:15:01.500
I'm giving it the name "g" and the&nbsp;
other part is purely in terms of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:06.000
Y. This would be the distribution for the Y&nbsp;
coordinate which I'm temporarily calling "h".

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:09.780
But if you combine this with the assumption&nbsp;
that things are radially symmetric,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.780 --> 00:15:14.220
both of these should be the same distribution.&nbsp;
The behavior on each axis should look the same.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.220 --> 00:15:19.620
So we could also write this as g(x)g(y).&nbsp;
It's the same function. And more than that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.620 --> 00:15:23.760
this function is actually going to be proportional&nbsp;
to the one we were just looking at. The one that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.760 --> 00:15:28.440
describes our probability density as a function&nbsp;
of the radius, the distance away from the origin.

00:15:28.440 --> 00:15:32.880
To see this, imagine you were to analyze a&nbsp;
point that was on the X axis, a distance r&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:37.320
away from the origin. Then the two distinct&nbsp;
ways to express our function based on the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.320 --> 00:15:42.840
different properties tells us that f(r) has&nbsp;
to equal some constant multiplied by g(r).

00:15:42.840 --> 00:15:47.640
So these functions f and g are basically the&nbsp;
same thing, just up to some constant multiple.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.840 --> 00:15:53.040
And you know what? It would be really nice&nbsp;
if we could just assume that that constant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.040 --> 00:15:57.540
was 1 so that f and g were literally the&nbsp;
same function and what I'm going to do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.540 --> 00:16:01.680
which might feel a little bit cheeky&nbsp;
is just assume that that is the case.

00:16:01.680 --> 00:16:05.100
What this means is that our answer is&nbsp;
going to be a little bit wrong. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.100 --> 00:16:08.700
function that we will deduce describing&nbsp;
this distribution will be off by some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.700 --> 00:16:13.260
constant factor. But that's no big deal&nbsp;
because in the end, we can just rescale to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.260 --> 00:16:17.040
make sure the area under the curve is 1 like&nbsp;
we always do with probability distributions.

00:16:17.040 --> 00:16:22.980
Now, if f and g are the same thing, this gives&nbsp;
us a very nice little equation purely in terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.980 --> 00:16:28.440
of the function f. Remember what this function&nbsp;
f is. If you have some point in the X Y plane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.440 --> 00:16:34.260
a distance r from the origin, then f(r) tells you&nbsp;
the relative likelihood of that point showing up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.260 --> 00:16:38.700
in the random process. More specifically, it&nbsp;
gives the probability density of that point.

00:16:38.700 --> 00:16:42.900
At the outset, this function could have&nbsp;
been anything. But Herschel's two different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.900 --> 00:16:47.100
properties evidently implies something kind of&nbsp;
funny about it. Which is that if we take the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:47.100 --> 00:16:51.900
X and Y coordinates of that point on the plane&nbsp;
and evaluate this function on them separately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.900 --> 00:16:57.360
taking f(x) times f(y) it should give&nbsp;
us the same result. Or if you prefer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.360 --> 00:17:00.600
we could expand out the meaning of&nbsp;
that distance r as the square root&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.600 --> 00:17:04.080
of X squared plus Y squared and this&nbsp;
is what our key equation looks like.

00:17:04.980 --> 00:17:09.780
This kind of equation is what's known in the&nbsp;
business as a Functional Equation. We're not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.780 --> 00:17:14.280
solving for an unknown number. Instead we're&nbsp;
saying that the equation is true for all possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.280 --> 00:17:21.240
numbers X and Y and the thing we're trying to find&nbsp;
is an unknown function. In the back of your mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.240 --> 00:17:25.860
you can think we already know one function that&nbsp;
satisfies this property, E to the negative X&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.860 --> 00:17:30.420
squared. And as a sanity check, you might&nbsp;
verify for yourself that it does satisfy that.

00:17:30.960 --> 00:17:35.520
Of course, the point is to pretend that you&nbsp;
don't know that and to instead deduce what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.520 --> 00:17:40.260
all of the functions are which satisfy this&nbsp;
property. In general, functional equations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.260 --> 00:17:45.060
can be quite tricky. But let me show you how&nbsp;
you can solve this one. First it's nice to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.060 --> 00:17:49.560
introduce a little helper function that I'll&nbsp;
call h(x) which will be defined as our mystery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.560 --> 00:17:55.620
function evaluated at the square root of X. Said&nbsp;
another way, h(x^2) is the same thing as f(x).

00:17:56.640 --> 00:18:01.380
For example, in the back of your mind where&nbsp;
you know that e^-x^2 will happen to be one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.380 --> 00:18:07.020
of the answers, this little helper function&nbsp;
h would be e^-x. But again, we're pretending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.020 --> 00:18:11.400
like we don't know that. The reason for doing&nbsp;
this is that the key property for f looks a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.400 --> 00:18:15.540
little bit nicer if we phrase it in terms of&nbsp;
this helper function, h. 'Cuz now what it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.540 --> 00:18:19.920
saying is if you take two arbitrary positive&nbsp;
numbers and you add them up and evaluate h,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.920 --> 00:18:24.480
it's the same thing as evaluating h on them&nbsp;
separately and then multiplying the results.

00:18:24.480 --> 00:18:30.180
In a sense, it turns addition into multiplication.&nbsp;
Some of you might see where this is going but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.180 --> 00:18:35.160
let's take a moment to walk through why&nbsp;
this forces our hand. As a next step,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:35.160 --> 00:18:39.000
you might want to pause and convince yourself&nbsp;
that if this property is true for the sum of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.000 --> 00:18:43.980
two numbers, this property also must be true&nbsp;
if we add up an arbitrary number of inputs.

00:18:45.180 --> 00:18:48.960
To get a feel for why this is so constraining,&nbsp;
think about plugging in a whole number,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.960 --> 00:18:54.720
something like h(5). Because you can write five&nbsp;
as one plus one plus one plus one plus one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.720 --> 00:19:00.060
this key property means that it must equal&nbsp;
h(1) multiplied by itself five times.

00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:05.760
Of course, there's nothing special about five, I&nbsp;
could have chosen any whole number N and we'd be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.760 --> 00:19:11.940
forced to that the function looks like some number&nbsp;
raised to the power N, and let's go ahead and give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.940 --> 00:19:17.700
that number a name like "b" for the base of our&nbsp;
exponential. As a little mini exercise here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.700 --> 00:19:21.060
see if you can pause and take a moment to&nbsp;
convince yourself that the same is true&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.060 --> 00:19:27.180
for a rational input. That if you plug in p/q to&nbsp;
this function, it must look like this base b^p/q.

00:19:30.540 --> 00:19:35.040
And as a hint, you might want to think about&nbsp;
adding that input to itself Q different times.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.460 --> 00:19:41.700
And then because rational numbers&nbsp;
are dense in the real number line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.700 --> 00:19:46.200
if we make one more pretty reasonable assumption&nbsp;
that we only care about continuous functions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.200 --> 00:19:49.380
this is enough to force your&nbsp;
hand completely and say that H&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:49.380 --> 00:19:54.600
has to be an exponential function,&nbsp;
b^x for all real number inputs x.

00:19:54.600 --> 00:19:57.180
I guess to be more precise, I should say for all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:57.180 --> 00:20:01.380
positive real inputs. The way we defined&nbsp;
h it's only taking in positive numbers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:02.280 --> 00:20:07.320
Now, as we've gone over before, instead of writing&nbsp;
down exponential functions as some base raised to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:07.320 --> 00:20:15.480
the power x, mathematicians often to write them&nbsp;
as e^cx. Making the choice to always use e as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:15.480 --> 00:20:19.620
base while letting that constant c determine&nbsp;
which specific exponential function you are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:19.620 --> 00:20:24.480
talking about just makes everything much easier&nbsp;
anytime calculus comes wandering along your path.

00:20:25.680 --> 00:20:30.120
And so, this means that our target&nbsp;
function f has to look like e^cx^2.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:33.480 --> 00:20:37.560
The beauty is that that function is no longer&nbsp;
something that was merely handed down to us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:37.560 --> 00:20:41.820
from on high. Instead, we started these&nbsp;
two different premises for how we wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:41.820 --> 00:20:46.500
a distribution in two dimensions to behave&nbsp;
and we were drawn to the conclusion that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:46.500 --> 00:20:50.400
shape of the expression describing that&nbsp;
distribution as a function of the radius&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:50.400 --> 00:20:55.320
away from the origin has to be e to the power&nbsp;
of some constant times that radius squared.

00:20:56.160 --> 00:20:59.820
You remember I said earlier this answer&nbsp;
will be off by a factor of a constant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:59.820 --> 00:21:04.140
We need to rescale it to make it a valid&nbsp;
probability distribution and geometrically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:04.140 --> 00:21:07.920
you might think of that as scaling it so that&nbsp;
the volume under the surface is equal to 1.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:08.760 --> 00:21:14.340
Now you notice that for positive values of this&nbsp;
constant in the exponent c, our function blows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:14.340 --> 00:21:18.480
up to infinity in all directions. So, the&nbsp;
volume under that surface would be infinite,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:18.480 --> 00:21:23.040
meaning it's not possible to renormalize. You&nbsp;
can't turn it into a probability distribution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:23.040 --> 00:21:28.140
and that leaves us with the last constraint which&nbsp;
is that this constant in the exponent has to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:28.140 --> 00:21:32.580
a negative number and the specific value of that&nbsp;
number determines the spread of the distribution.

00:21:33.780 --> 00:21:38.160
10 years after Herschel wrote this, James&nbsp;
Clerk Maxwell, who's most well known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:38.160 --> 00:21:41.340
for having written down the fundamental&nbsp;
equations for electricity and magnetism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:41.340 --> 00:21:45.840
independently stumbled across the same&nbsp;
derivation. In his case, he was doing it in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:45.840 --> 00:21:50.160
three dimensions since he was doing statistical&nbsp;
mechanics and he was deriving a formula for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:50.160 --> 00:21:55.140
distribution for velocities of molecules in&nbsp;
a gas, but the logic all works out the same.

00:21:55.800 --> 00:22:00.180
For you and me, if we view this as&nbsp;
the defining property of a Gaussian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:00.180 --> 00:22:04.500
then it's a little bit less surprising that&nbsp;
pi might make an appearance. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:04.500 --> 00:22:09.600
circular symmetry was part of this defining&nbsp;
property. More than that, it makes the clever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:09.600 --> 00:22:14.820
proof that we saw earlier feel a little bit less&nbsp;
out of the blue. I mean, a key problem solve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:14.820 --> 00:22:20.700
principle in math is to use the defining features&nbsp;
of your setup. And if you had been primed by this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:20.700 --> 00:22:25.500
Hershel/Maxwell derivation where the defining&nbsp;
property for a Gaussian is this coincidence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:25.500 --> 00:22:30.780
of having a distribution that's both radially&nbsp;
symmetric and also independent along each axis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:30.780 --> 00:22:36.300
Then the very first step of our proof which seemed&nbsp;
so strange bumping the problem up one dimension&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:36.300 --> 00:22:41.160
was really just a way of opening the door to&nbsp;
let that defining property make itself visible.

00:22:42.060 --> 00:22:47.100
And if you think back, the essence of the came&nbsp;
down to using that radial symmetry on the one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:47.100 --> 00:22:50.640
hand and then also using the ability&nbsp;
to factor the function on the other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:51.240 --> 00:22:54.120
From this standpoint, using both those facts feels&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:54.120 --> 00:22:58.320
less like a trick that happens to work&nbsp;
and more like an inevitable necessity.

00:23:00.120 --> 00:23:03.540
Nevertheless, thinking once again&nbsp;
of our statistician's friend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:03.540 --> 00:23:09.360
this is still not entirely satisfying. Using&nbsp;
the Herschel/Maxwell derivation, saying this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:09.360 --> 00:23:14.100
property of a multi-dimensional distribution is&nbsp;
what defines a Gaussian, well that presumes that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:14.100 --> 00:23:19.080
we're already in some kind of multi-dimensional&nbsp;
situation in the first place. Much more commonly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:19.080 --> 00:23:24.660
the way that a normal distribution arises in&nbsp;
practice doesn't feel spatial or geometric at all.

00:23:24.660 --> 00:23:29.100
It stems from the central limit theorem which&nbsp;
is all about adding together many different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:29.100 --> 00:23:33.600
independent variables. So to bring it all&nbsp;
home here, what we need to do is explain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:33.600 --> 00:23:38.160
why the function that's characterized by&nbsp;
this Herschel/Maxwell derivation should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:38.160 --> 00:23:42.000
be the same thing as the function that sits&nbsp;
at the heart of the central limit theorem.

00:23:42.660 --> 00:23:47.280
And at this point, those of you following along&nbsp;
are probably going to make fun of me. I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:47.280 --> 00:23:53.040
it makes sense to pull this last step as its own&nbsp;
video. I realise this is what? The third time in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:53.040 --> 00:23:57.120
a row that I've kicked this particular can down&nbsp;
